now just a day to go before elections in  the u.s  president donald trump and his  democratic rival joe biden have  been making their final pitches to  voters trump has been holding campaign  rallies in five battleground states  meanwhile biden's campaign is focused on  pennsylvania  trump believes he can pull off another  surprise victory as he did four years  ago  most polls though have been showing  biden ahead in key states  needed to win  well many eyes will be on the biggest  swing state of all  florida it's been seen as a must-win for  donald trump  the past two elections have seen a  wafer-thin margin between the winner and  loser there  president trump is banking on the  support of one of the state's  main voting blocks the latino vote  dw's carolina chimoy has been to find  out  how the republicans under president  trump have tapped into a narrative  that's found appeal among florida's  cuban americans  they want to see donald trump stay in  the white house  and they are determined to deliver  florida for him  it's a must win state for the presidency  even at this scaled-back democrat rally  in orlando  the trump voters are far louder  trump has strong support from a large  part of the latino community here  in florida this despite things which  would seem almost designed to put  latinos off  like the harsh words he has used to  describe immigrants  i'm here to find out why the hispanic  vote leads more republican  here in florida nearly a third of the  latino voters in florida  have cuban roots including bertica  cabrera morris  head of latinos for trump in orlando she  was 15 when her family fled cuba  after losing everything to the  government of ivel castro they just  knocked one day at the door and they  said  it's ours now we had lost everything you  know we had lost our beautiful home  the ranch the chauffeur but we had a  different life  and you adapt that's why voting is so  important to me  because it's a gift that this country  has given me that i didn't have  i don't they took away one of her  biggest fears like many other cuban  americans  is the idea that the system they fled  could be adopted in the united states  no i don't think joe biden is a  socialist  or a communist but i do believe the  people around him are  and they have the same socialist ideas  that i lived through in cuba  and i've seen in venezuela so yes i do  believe that he surrounded  with that and a leader is as good as the  people around them  being pro-trump is slowly becoming  something the president's supporters  keep to themselves  because there can be consequences miker  plummer  is also a devoted trump supporter so i  have a president for you  what's that i have um all right  um supporting our president yeah the  last job the last job i was at  he says you're not a trump fan i said no  why because  i wouldn't let you do the job if you  were a trump fan so i told her i was a  biden fan just to do the job  and that's the only way i could get to  work  it's not just the fear of socialism  driving support for trump  latino voters are catholic and support  the republican position  against abortion and it may seem  counter-intuitive  but many also support trump's heart line  on immigration  even from latin american countries  trump tells the truth i don't want other  hispanics to come into the u.s  even if they are from my country peru if  they don't pay taxes  that is not fair so if more hispanics  come and don't pay taxes they seal  and leave off the money they get from  the government then i'm a racist too  and i also say get out it's not fair  that those that come through the border  illegally paying a coyote ten thousand  dollars  to come here and we don't know who they  are they could be rapists  here in florida support for president  trump and the republicans  is deeply emotional if that support is  reflected at the ballot box  it could decide the election immigration  policy will be a major issue for voters  appearing to get tough on immigration  has been at the heart of donald trump's  presidency  the main agency in charge of deporting  people without legal documents is the  u.s  immigration and customs enforcement  known as ice  and many live in fear of its reach dw  correspondent carolina chimoy sent us  this report  from the state of georgia  nichols is a defender of immigrants  rights she wants them to know that they  don't need to hide where they come from  her organization glare the georgia  latino alliance for human rights  has been working on this for 19 years  until recently  in dialogue with the federal government  but that channel of communication to  talk about  immigration is cut off by the trump  administration she says  obama deported a lot of our people  however  there was room to negotiate immigration  policies  it was a political fight  now on a federal level that is not  possible anymore  this is why they now work on a local  level going from door to door to  motivate people to vote  they hope to elect politicians who will  work with them on immigration reform  and prevent deportations this man from  central america who wants to remain  anonymous  had lived in the u.s for six years with  his family  then one day in 2012 he was stopped by  ice agents  they took him to the irwin county  detention center  there everyone cries even the strongest  men cry  i saw it a big man another one with a  mustache and all crying  because there is no certainty that  you'll ever get out  after three months in detention he was  released with legal help from adelina's  group  who showed he had no criminal background  under the current administration his  release would not have been possible  the american dream doesn't exist anymore  today it's more an american nightmare  especially with this president  he now constantly fears he could be  arrested again  on any given day around 50 000 people  are in migration detention in the united  states  human rights advocates report cruel  conditions  and they say it has even gotten worse  under the trump administration  a system that is unlikely to change  overnight even with a different  administration the change at the top  could at least reopen that door to  dialogue  [Applause]  let's bring in william gluecroft here  from our u.s election team  good to see you so uh florida  very complicated demographically how  important  is it in this election complicated is a  nice way of putting it it's often the  butt of the joe of a joke in america  politically socially culturally i mean  there are social media accounts that  follow all of just the crazy news that  sometimes come out comes out of florida  that said it is an extraordinarily  important election almost  every winning president in recent  history has won florida  and you can't really win the presidency  without winning florida and it's such a  diverse  state 17 of registered voters in florida  are now  latino which is a huge amount of  voters but also we have a huge  retirement population we have  rural white voters you have a large  jewish population  um you have an increasingly young  population  all of these are competing groups and  you know we talk about latino  but that in with itself is a diverse  group we're talking about people coming  from dozens of countries or their  previous generations have come from  dozens of countries with hundreds of  experiences  you know when we talk about for example  in comparison black americans  80 to 90 percent of black americans vote  democrat they are much more reliable  in politically speaking electorally  speaking a much more uniform group of  people  when we're talking about latinos it's  important to say  uh we saw in the report many trump  supporters but the majority are still in  florida going to go  for biden but you do have a breakdown  where there are  more people voting for trump we saw this  in 2016. you had  uh 35 percent of all latinos voting  for donald trump but 54 of cubans within  that group 